          body:
            "Two functions solve the same problem. One takes 3 milliseconds, the other 10 — on the laptop you measured today. Which is better? The question is malformed, because neither machine nor input size was held constant, and the *shape* of the two curves answers a question the stopwatch cannot: **what happens as the input grows?**\n\nA function that takes 10 ms but triples with every doubling of input will beat a 3 ms function that *squares* with every doubling, once the data reaches the sizes real systems reach. At n = 1,000 the linear one is still trivial; at n = 1,000,000 the quadratic one is doing a trillion operations — a thousand years of work on the same hardware. Big-O notation is how you say this *before* running it: it classifies how an algorithm's cost grows, without pretending to predict its speed on any particular machine.\n\nThat separation is the practical point. **Big-O ignores hardware** (a laptop and a server have different constants) **and predicts at scale** (growth is a property of the algorithm alone). When you say \"lookup in a hash map is O(1)\", you are making a claim that holds on every machine — that the cost per item stays roughly flat as the table grows. When you say \"this nested loop is O(n²)\", you are predicting the day the dataset doubles and the job quadruples. The notation exists so that conversation can happen in a meeting, on a whiteboard, before the invoice arrives.",
