            "Analyse this exactly — count, do not guess:\n\n```js\nfunction mystery(items, target) {\n  const seen = new Set();                    // 1\n  for (const item of items) {                // n iterations\n    if (seen.has(target - item)) return true;//   Θ(1) average — hash lookup\n    seen.add(item);                          //   Θ(1) average\n  }\n  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++)     // n iterations\n    for (let j = i; j < items.length; j++)   // (n-i) iterations\n      if (items[i] + items[j] === target) return true;\n  return false;\n}\n```\n\n**Step 1 — sum the parts.** Line 1: constant. Loop A: *n* iterations × Θ(1) each = Θ(n). Loop B: the triangle sum = *n(n+1)/2* = Θ(n²). Total: *T(n) = c₁ + c₂·n + n(n+1)/2*.\n\n**Step 2 — keep the dominant term.** As *n* grows, the *n²/2* term swamps the linear one: **Θ(n²)** (written O(n²)). The constant ½ disappears with everything else.\n\n**Step 3 — state the assumptions honestly.** The first loop's `Set.has` is Θ(1) *on average*; a pathological hash distribution could degrade to Θ(n) per call, making the first loop Θ(n²). The standard phrasing: \"O(n) average, O(n²) worst-case for the hash degradation — but the second loop is Θ(n²) regardless, so the overall worst case is Θ(n²) either way.\"\n\n**Step 4 — translate into a decision.** At *n* = 10,000 items, the second loop does ~50,000,000 comparisons — about a second of JavaScript's hot loop. At *n* = 1,000,000 it does ~5×10¹¹ — hours. **The algorithm's shape, not the machine, sets the deadline.** Now look at the first loop: it solves the *same* two-sum problem in Θ(n) with a set of complements. The function contains a linear solution and a quadratic one — the analysis just told you which half to delete:\n\n```js\nfunction hasPairWithSum(items, target) {\n  const seen = new Set();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (seen.has(target - item)) return true;\n    seen.add(item);\n  }\n  return false;\n}                                              // Θ(n) — one pass, one lookup each\n```\n\n**The final habit**: after naming the complexity, sanity-check it empirically — time both versions at n = 1,000, 2,000, 4,000. Quadratic should ~4× per doubling; linear should ~2×. The ratio *is* the growth rate, visible on a clock.",
