          heading: "Common mistakes and misconceptions",
          kind: "warning",
          body:
            "- **Quoting Big-O as speed.** O(n²) with n = 10 is 100 operations; O(n) with n = 10⁹ is a billion. Notation compares *shapes*, not absolute cost — always pair it with the n you expect.\n- **Dropping constants too early in conversation.** Two 50× constants still matter when choosing between two Θ(n) implementations — notation discards them, engineering must not.\n- **Saying O(n²) when you mean Θ(n²).** O is an upper bound and technically allows looser answers; tight bounds are Θ. Colloquially everyone means tight — until the ambiguity matters in an exam or a design review.\n- **Forgetting the loop counter's step.** `i *= 2` is logarithmic, not linear. Read how the index *changes*, not just the limit.\n- **Treating a library call as O(1)** because it is one line: `.sort()` is Θ(n log n), `.includes()` is Θ(n), `indexOf` is Θ(n). Inside a loop, their cost multiplies.\n- **Ignoring best/worst/average distinction.** Hash tables: O(1) average, O(n) worst. Quote both when the worst case is plausible.\n- **Two inputs collapsed to one variable.** `n + m` is not `n·m`. If two collections each of size n, say so explicitly.\n- **Believing amortised ≠ real.** The occasional Θ(n) array resize is real and can appear as a latency spike; for hard real-time work you pre-allocate — but for typical workloads, amortised Θ(1) is the honest number.\n- **Analysing before correctness.** A fast wrong answer is not an algorithm. Prove it works (or test it hard) first; then analyse its cost.\n- **Micro-optimising O(n) code while shipping O(n²).** Big-O first, constants later — in that order, always. Fixing the growth class beats shaving 30% off a doomed shape.",
