          body:
            "A graph is vertices and edges; a tree is the special case with unique paths, which is why tree traversals need no visited set and graph traversals cannot do without one. DFS explores depth-first with a stack and yields pre/post/in orders (in-order reads a BST sorted; reversed post-order is a topological sort); BFS explores level-by-level with a queue and gives shortest paths by hop count the moment it first sees a node. Both cost **Θ(V + E)** on an adjacency list, which is the default representation because graphs are sparse. Traversal is the skeleton under everything else: cycle detection adds three colours, topological sort adds post-order reversal, reachability adds a visited check, and Dijkstra replaces the queue's FIFO order with a cost order — at which point the weights start to matter. Choose by asking what *distance* means to your problem, watch the two implementation traps (enqueue-time marking, `shift`'s cost), and state your representation whenever you quote a complexity.",
